<h1>ECON 8310: Business Forecasting (with AI)</h1>
<div class="byline">University of Nebraska at Omaha &middot; Department of Economics</div>
<div class="byline"><span id="updated">Last updated: {{UPDATED}}</span></div>

<p style="margin-top:1.6em">Forecasting tools and applications for business settings. The first
half of the course covers traditional econometric forecasting; the second half turns to
predictive analytics and machine learning. Everything is implemented in Python.</p>

<h2>Course documents</h2>

<p><a href="files/about.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>About this course</strong></a> &mdash; what it teaches,
what it assumes you already know, and the books it reads from.</p>

<div class="callout amber">
  <span class="t">Enrolled at UNO? Canvas has what this site does not</span>
  <p>This site carries the <strong>course materials</strong> &mdash; every slide deck, lab, assignment,
  the book and the workbook &mdash; and nothing specific to one university, term or section. It has no
  calendar dates, no grading scale, no lecture recordings and no course policies.</p>
  <p>If you are enrolled, <strong>Canvas is authoritative</strong> for all of those: your syllabus, the
  term calendar and due dates, the lecture videos, the academic-integrity and accessibility policies,
  and how work is submitted and graded. Where the two ever disagree, Canvas wins.</p>
</div>

<p>The rest of the course documents:</p>

{{DOCUMENTS_TABLE}}

<h2>Textbooks</h2>

<p>All are free online. None needs to be purchased.</p>

{{TEXTBOOKS_TABLE}}

<h2>Before the first lab</h2>

<p>Every lab and homework in this course reads from prepared data files that are <em>not</em> in the
repository. Run these three commands once, from the repository root, before Week 1:</p>

<pre><code>python scripts/prep_m5.py            # M5 / Walmart — the course spine
python scripts/prep_fred.py          # FRED macro series — Lecture 2
python scripts/prep_electricity.py   # hourly electricity demand — Lecture 3</code></pre>

<p>Each downloads from a public source, needs no login or API key, and writes to
<code>data/processed/</code>. Once they have run, nothing in this course needs the network again.</p>

<div class="callout amber">
  <span class="t">macOS users: one extra step before Week 5</span>
  <p>XGBoost will not import on macOS without the OpenMP runtime. Install it once with
  <code>brew install libomp</code>, then <code>pip install xgboost</code>. Do this before Lab 5 rather
  than during it.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout"><span class="t">New to Quarto? Start here</span>
  <p><a href="files/setup-guide.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Setup and Quarto guide</strong></a> &mdash; installing
  Python, Quarto and an editor (VS Code or Positron), what a <code>.qmd</code> file is, and the four
  errors you will hit. <strong>Google Colab will not work</strong> &mdash; it cannot open
  <code>.qmd</code> files.</p>
  <p>When something breaks later &mdash; a package that will not install, a render that fails, a
  lab that errors halfway &mdash; go to <a href="files/troubleshooting.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Troubleshooting</strong></a>
  first. It covers the failures this course actually produces.</p>
</div>

<p>You will also need: <code>jupyter</code>, <code>pandas</code>, <code>numpy</code>,
<code>matplotlib</code>, <code>statsmodels</code>, <code>scikit-learn</code>, <code>prophet</code>,
<code>pygam</code>, <code>xgboost</code>, <code>torch</code>, <code>pymc</code>, and <code>arviz</code>.
<code>jupyter</code> is not optional &mdash; Quarto runs your Python through a Jupyter kernel. Assignment submission, the AI workflow, and the prompt log live in the
<a href="https://github.com/DataHurdler/Forecasting-Env" target="_blank" rel="noopener">course environment repository</a>.</p>

<h2>Weekly schedule</h2>

<p class="neutral" style="font-size:.95em;color:#525252">The sixteen <strong>teaching weeks</strong>, in
order, with the lecture each one covers. There are no calendar dates here: those belong to a
particular term and section, and a break week falls in a different place each semester. Enrolled
students get the dated calendar &mdash; including breaks and due dates &mdash; from Canvas, where the
week numbers match these.</p>

{{SCHEDULE}}

<h2>Assignments</h2>

{{ASSIGNMENTS_TABLE}}


<div class="callout">
  <span class="t">Each assignment has a prompt budget</span>
  <p>Every assignment carries a prompt budget and an initial context prompt that does not count
  against it. Any AI assistant is fine &mdash; ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot &mdash; in an editor or a
  browser tab. UNO's ChatGPT Edu workspace does not include Codex, so nothing here requires it. You submit a <code>PROMPT_LOG.md</code> alongside your rendered
  work, listing every prompt you sent in order.</p>
  <p>The budget is a target, not a limit. It exists to make you decide what is worth asking, and
  you may go over it &mdash; log the extra prompts and say where you got stuck. What does cost you
  is a log that does not match the work you submit.</p>
</div>

<h2>The course as a book</h2>

<p>Every lecture, in one continuous document you can read, search and quote from &mdash; the slides
and what was said about them, assembled into fifteen numbered chapters. Useful for revision, for
reading ahead, and for finding the lecture where a method was introduced without opening five decks.</p>

<p><a href="book/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Read the book &rarr;</strong></a></p>

<h2>The labs and assignments as a workbook</h2>

<p>The companion volume: all fourteen labs and eleven assignments in one searchable document. Code
is shown rather than run, so it opens instantly &mdash; each lab links to its executable version for
when you are actually working. Every step carries a collapsible walkthrough of what was said about
it in class, and every lab ends with the output your own run should produce.</p>

<p><a href="workbook/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Open the workbook &rarr;</strong></a></p>

<div class="callout">
  <span class="t">It is generated, not written separately</span>
  <p>The book is assembled from the decks themselves, so a table, figure or number in it cannot
  disagree with the lecture it came from. If a deck is corrected, the book carries the correction
  the next time it is built.</p>
</div>


